WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.440
- Good morning.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.460
Welcome, everyone, to
day two of our workshop.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.620
Yesterday was absolutely fantastic

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.830
and not only did I learn a lot,

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:21.540
but I think we managed to
break many language barriers

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.390
as it were in trying to
understand the concepts

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:28.080
and models that people
across disciplines use

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.930
when thinking about the kinds of inference

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:34.260
and the sort of pitfalls in
many of the methodologies

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.500
that we are using, the
limitations across fields.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:42.500
So it is my great pleasure
today to introduce

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:48.930
our Dean of Faculty of the
Faculty of Arts and Sciences,

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.100
Professor Tamar Gendler,

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:55.020
who is an exemplar of
interdisciplinary scholarship

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.660
and whose support, I mean in her own work

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:02.040
she has worked across
multiple feats, philosophy,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:07.040
psychology, cognitive
neuroscience, and is very committed

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:12.630
as, you know, an administrator
in the university,

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:15.840
she has been really heralding
sort of intellectual crossing

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.900
of boundaries and many initiatives

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.530
and programs to really
facilitate conversations.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.300
As you all know, universities,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.833
though we are all in
with various expertise

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.680
that often speaks to very
similar sets of problems

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.870
and challenges that we work on,

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.520
we don't get to talk as much.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.730
So with the support of the Franke Program

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:42.990
in Science and the Humanities

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:47.990
that Yale has very enthusiastically,
not just supported,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.110
but encouraged faculty to
attend and be involved,

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:56.550
Tamar has, as I said, in her
own scholarship and beyond,

00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:00.690
really been a steward for
cross-disciplinary work.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:03.630
So it is my absolute delight

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.840
to invite her to say a few words.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.120
Tamar, thank you so much for coming.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.865
- [Tamar] Thank you so much, Priya.

00:02:10.865 --> 00:02:13.830
(attendees clapping)

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:16.320
Thank you, all, and
I'm sorry I wasn't here

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.780
to greet you yesterday morning.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.780
These are, as you know,
challenging times for universities.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:28.890
I was in New York helping
some of our colleagues

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:32.880
and supporters understand
how hard we're trying.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.690
But though these are trying
times for universities,

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:40.470
this conference is an example

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:45.470
of what makes universities truly distinct

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:50.220
and what it is about the
imaginative enterprise

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.940
of creating an institutional structure

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:58.860
that is simultaneously
engaged in the preservation,

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.860
the creation, and the
transmission of knowledge.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:09.120
And in my mind, this conference epitomizes

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.660
what is great about universities.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:17.660
It is asking a deep and
intellectual question

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.950
that is simultaneously of theoretical

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:25.320
and practical significance.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:29.580
It is distinct, I think,
to be able to have

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:33.810
a series of events that
simultaneously engage

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:38.810
some of the deepest and most
abstract philosophical work,

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:42.570
really about the nature of understanding

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.430
in its most fundamental sense,

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:47.960
and that simultaneously brings in,

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:51.360
in very real practical ways

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.573
a set of empirical disciplines.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:00.420
It's rare to find a topic
that is not at the periphery

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.170
of one field and the center of another,

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:09.170
but at the center of every
field of which it is a part.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:14.070
And it is rare to find a subject

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:17.520
that though centrally part of each field

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:22.320
and common enough to
merit joint discussion

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:27.320
is differentially illuminated
by different methodologies.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:31.890
So that's some theoretical
reasons to think

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:36.890
this series of events is doing
what a university can do.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:42.990
But I also wanna point out,
because I have a second hat,

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:47.190
I'm not just responsible
for intellectual matters,

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:51.300
though I take great joy in
reading the academic work

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.640
of my colleagues regularly.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:56.040
The second thing I wanna emphasize

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:01.040
is that this series of events
of which all of you are a part

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:06.350
took advantage of structural
features of the university,

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.930
of structural features of the ecosystem

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:11.520
of which we are all apart,

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.060
and to the extent that the
group I am addressing is a set

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:19.740
of stewards of the intellect
at their own universities.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:21.510
I wanna point out to you

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:25.113
some of the practical
features of this event.

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:30.180
This project was carried out over years.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.640
It used multiple modalities.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.340
It took advantage of the fact

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:37.680
that people can drop in and out

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:42.390
and nonetheless be part of a
loosely knit social network.

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:45.270
For each of the events
that it carried out,

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:47.820
it had it both in real time

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:51.390
and available for subsequent observation.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:54.270
It brought unexpected groups of people

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:56.550
into interaction with one another.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:06:00.360
It took advantage of
the funding environment

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:02.310
in which we find ourselves.

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:05.340
The Templeton Foundation
is deeply interested

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:09.180
in interdisciplinary issues
in fundamental questions.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:13.290
It supplemented those resources
with an entity on campus,

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:18.210
the Franke, that had already
established a mailing list

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:22.350
and a brand that allowed
these events to be legible.

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:27.350
And it involved a wide range
of faculty on and off campus.

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:29.640
Priya did the very smart thing

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.640
of very early alerting this to me.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.350
And so when I went around

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:36.750
and met with supporters of the university,

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:39.570
this was one of the things I talked about.

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:42.480
Every single thing that
Priya did with regard

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:46.140
to this event is something
that every single one of you

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:48.660
can do at your home institution.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.450
Think about what are the networks

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:55.440
that have already given
us a pre-established list

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.560
of individuals with interest
in a large range of questions?

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:01.950
Who are the nodes at your university

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:03.930
that if you tell them about this,

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:06.810
they will tell the world about this?

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:11.810
This is not only in your
self-interest as scholars.

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:17.030
The university in the world
is an entity that is at risk.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:21.960
It is being challenged for
reasons, some appropriate,

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:23.700
some inappropriate.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:28.110
But the more we focus and
celebrate core mission,

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:31.380
the more likely it is the
universities will remain

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:34.890
the entities that they have long been.

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:36.720
So that's where I was yesterday.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:38.700
I was in defend the university space

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.590
and I was writing this on the train

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:43.230
and so I wrote you a little
defend the university.

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:48.230
But I also wanna do a couple
of specific reflections

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:53.540
on the topic that this group
has been thinking about.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.100
And then I'm gonna just
close by reading a poem

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.320
because this doesn't have anybody

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:00.330
from the English
department and so I thought

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:02.970
I'd give you a little bit
of literature at the end.

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:06.900
So on the front page of the webpage,

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:09.690
which I assume is the
cover page of the proposal

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:10.920
that you sent to Templeton,

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:13.620
it certainly feels like the
cover page of a proposal

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:15.990
that one would send to Templeton,

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:20.197
is the incredibly resonant
quote from Hume that,

00:08:20.197 --> 00:08:23.580
"No amount of observation of white swans

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:25.980
can allow the inference
that all swans are white,

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:29.340
but the observation of a single black swan

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:32.850
is sufficient to refute that conclusion."

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:35.550
And I don't get to do a lot of philosophy,

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:38.730
I don't get to do a lot
of reflecting on texts

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:41.070
so it was a total pleasure

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:45.870
just to live in this
quotation and its depth.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:49.770
And I just wanna give
voice to three thoughts

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:52.140
that occurred to me.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:56.250
One, I had not realized till I started

00:08:56.250 --> 00:09:00.210
thinking about this quote,
that one way to describe

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:05.210
what almost every academic
does is to figure out

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:09.210
which features of the
world that seem shallow

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:12.540
are actually deep and
which features of the world

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:16.890
that seem deep are actually shallow.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:19.500
That is, for example, the project

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:23.370
of our Department of Women's,
Gender, and Sexuality Studies

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:26.400
which aspects of the
world that seem universal

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:29.220
may be particular, which
aspects of the world

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:32.880
that seem particular may
actually have deep foundations

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:36.000
as I suspect we will
hear in our next talk.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.260
But over and over and over
again, including in the work

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:43.080
that we do in engineering, which ways

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:46.170
in which this substance feels brittle

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:50.070
have to do with deep
features of its structure,

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:53.700
which are merely manifestations
of the idiosyncratic way

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:55.260
in which we find it?

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:57.210
So that's just thought one.

00:09:57.210 --> 00:10:02.210
Hume tells us a way to think
about the questions that we ask

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:07.860
is to think about where we are
mistaking surface for depth,

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:11.250
where we are mistaking depth for surface.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:15.270
Ultimately, a platonic
question about our capacity

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:17.283
to understand the nature of reality.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:21.510
Two, if Hume had lived in Australia,

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:25.500
it wouldn't have occurred to
him that all swans were white.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:29.100
In Australia, lots of swans are black.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:33.840
It is contingent what it is
that we take to be ordinary

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.530
and what it is that we take to be unusual.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:42.530
That reminder is crucial for
all of our investigation.

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:44.670
That is not a political statement,

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:47.370
it's an epistemic fact about the world

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:52.370
that all of us are only partially
exposed to elements of it

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:56.940
and on that basis we draw conclusions.

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:01.590
Note that as we build artificial analogs

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:06.590
to our own intelligence, they
too don't live in Australia.

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:10.770
That is to say they
too are getting exposed

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:14.580
only to that which is
electronically represented.

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:19.580
And just like Hume, who
was very, very smart,

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:24.960
they might fail to
recognize how idiosyncratic

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:29.760
the part of the world to which
they have been exposed is.

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:31.443
Third point.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:38.040
How do we gather
information about the world?

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:43.040
One of the questions that this
assembly is thinking about

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:48.050
is the entire set of
methodological worries that arise.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:51.330
To what extent do we seek information?

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:53.760
To what extent does seeking information

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:55.980
distort the information
that we're able to get?

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:59.130
How do we become active perceivers?

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:03.330
I spend a lot of time, I
oversee all of the tenure

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:05.910
and promotion cases that come before us,

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:08.790
I see the methodologies
that ethnography brings us,

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:11.400
which says, sit back, don't theorize

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:13.890
and you will see things more truly.

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:18.890
I see the methodology that's
brought by areas of inquiry

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:23.340
that say if you do not have
a theoretical framework,

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:28.340
you will mistake what is
important for what is unimportant.

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:33.510
Both those things are true

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:37.230
and the methodological
diversity of this event

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:41.340
seems to me to capture
that in profound ways.

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:44.460
All right, those are
three thoughts on Hume.

00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:49.460
Let me close with a wonderful
poem by Mary Oliver.

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:52.680
This is not a hard,
deep poem to understand.

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:57.510
This is just a beautiful
celebration of our experience

00:12:57.510 --> 00:12:59.647
as humans on earth.

00:12:59.647 --> 00:13:01.507
"The Swan."

00:13:01.507 --> 00:13:06.507
"Did you see it too, drifting
all night on the black river?

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:09.210
Did you see it in the morning,

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:14.160
rising into the silvery air,
an arm full of white blossoms,

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:17.700
a perfect commotion of silk

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:22.700
and linen as it leaned into
the bondage of its wings?

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:28.640
A snow bank, a bank of
lilies, biting the air

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:30.990
with its black beak.

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:33.390
Did you hear it fluting and whistling,

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:37.560
a shrill dark music, like
the rain pelting the trees,

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:41.190
like a waterfall knifing
down the black ledges?

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:45.870
And did you see it finally
just under the clouds,

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:49.650
a white cross streaming across the sky,

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:51.750
its feet like black leaves,

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:55.350
its wings like the stretching
light of the river?

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:58.080
And did you feel it in your heart

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:02.460
how it pertained to everything?

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:07.000
And have you finally figured
out what beauty is for

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:10.947
and have you changed your life?"

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:16.168
Thank you so much for inviting
me in for your assembly.

00:14:16.168 --> 00:14:19.251
(attendees clapping)

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:25.920
- Thanks, Tamar for that
very, very lovely welcome

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:28.650
and thanks to all the
attendees who are here.

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:31.410
So we are opening this morning's session

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:32.490
with the first talk.

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:34.320
Eduardo, take it away.

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:35.153
- Thank you.

00:14:38.453 --> 00:14:39.286
Can you hear me there?

00:14:39.286 --> 00:14:40.119
Yes.

00:14:40.119 --> 00:14:41.090
Well, good morning everyone.

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:43.443
I wanna stay on time.

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:47.910
Thank you, Priya, for inviting me.

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:50.640
I'll have a few words to
really share with you all.

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:52.470
Why is it that I'm here?

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:54.900
Thanks, Tamar, I'll
have some comments about

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:57.000
what you were referring to before.

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:58.170
You have the title there.

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:01.710
We're gonna move from to
look into some different type

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:04.800
of causation, causation,
the way we examine it

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:06.780
in biological anthropology.

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:10.650
And I will say more
about that in a minute.

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:13.890
What I wanna do first is
get the clicker to work.

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:14.820
There you go.

00:15:14.820 --> 00:15:16.560
I think it is important I
put together this slide,

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:18.390
which I don't usually show

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:21.360
to give you some background
on my own background, right?

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:22.530
I'm not a philosopher.

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:24.030
That much you've learned from yesterday.

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:26.820
I'm not a computer scientist,
I'm not a statistician.

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:29.430
I'm a biologist by training

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:32.040
and with focus on behavioral sciences

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:34.437
and that has got me to
move across disciplines.

00:15:34.437 --> 00:15:37.590
And I really think that
maybe it's helped me

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:40.050
to really appreciate and
embrace the opportunity

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:42.600
that this workshop is
bringing to all of us.

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:46.230
My very first research
paper was working in a lab

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:49.080
that studies the neurobiology
of learning and memory.

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:50.850
We were using some short crops

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:53.250
that we were collecting from
the beaches in Buenos Aires,

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:55.740
bringing them to the lab,
giving them electric shocks

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:57.360
to see if they could learn or not.

00:15:57.360 --> 00:15:58.980
They don't.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:00.390
But then I went on to the PhD

00:16:00.390 --> 00:16:03.060
to work with some
experimental psychologists,

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:06.930
so I went from neurobiology
to experimental psychology,

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:08.250
tackling some of the same questions

