00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:11.340
but we just need to get more money.

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:15.900
And so in 2019, Dean
Gendler launched a program

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:19.260
that was absolutely brilliant,
I thank, it continues to run,

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:21.810
which was the Teaching
Relief for Learning.

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:24.090
We were invited to come up with an idea,

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:25.260
we needed to convince them

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:29.370
that we could make good use
of that time to take courses

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:33.210
instead of teaching courses,
go back to the classroom.

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:35.580
And that was a time when
I was starting to to say,

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:39.360
listen, we're not reflecting
on what we have learned

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:42.240
in a causal inference sense
within my own research.

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:45.570
I mean, I have been doing my
research now for 25 years,

00:18:45.570 --> 00:18:49.050
so I knew that some of the
social sciences were developing

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:52.920
all kinds of methodological
tools to get at causality.

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:56.760
And so I literally enrolled
in a research methods course

00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:00.300
in causal inference, taught
in political sciences,

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:01.740
and then I took an undergraduate course,

00:19:01.740 --> 00:19:04.980
one of our survey
introductory courses in stats.

00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:08.910
And I did both and I completed
my homework and I passed.

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:12.480
It was fantastic, it was
fantastic because I realized that,

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:15.840
oh, my, do we need to
bring some of those tools

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:17.880
into biological anthropology?

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:18.840
I continued working this.

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:20.280
There are all kinds of developments

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:21.990
that I'd be happy to share with you

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:24.750
because like Tamar said,
maybe some of these ideas,

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:26.460
you'll bring to your institutions.

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:28.350
But it was only a few months ago

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:31.230
as I was developing a new
course on causal inference

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:33.870
that I just went onto the Yale website

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:35.670
and typed causal inference.

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:37.800
And I will admit here right now,

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:40.590
that I only learned about
the causal inference project

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:41.970
that we have at our institution

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:44.147
maybe three, four months ago.

00:19:44.147 --> 00:19:47.640
And yes, the more you
work, the luckier you get.

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:51.747
I emailed Priya and I said,
I wanna know more about this.

00:19:51.747 --> 00:19:53.280
And that got us talking

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:56.877
and I jumped on the wagon of
what Priya has been doing.

00:19:56.877 --> 00:19:58.530
And so that's why I'm here,

00:19:58.530 --> 00:20:00.990
only because I was seeking information,

00:20:00.990 --> 00:20:04.830
I was looking for ways
in which I could expand

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:06.960
my intellectual interest

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:09.690
and prepare myself better
for doing my research.

00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:12.123
So that's my history of why I'm here.

00:20:13.639 --> 00:20:14.472
A little bit of the history,

00:20:14.472 --> 00:20:16.710
I mean, biology has a long
history of of thinking

00:20:16.710 --> 00:20:18.960
and contributing to the
development of statistics.

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:21.870
I mean, this is just two
graphs from a wonderful book

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:23.970
that most of you probably
know, "The Book of Why"

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:26.880
by Judea Pearl, is Sir Francis Galton

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:31.080
towards the end of the 19th
century, just developing ideas

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:35.880
for explaining the inheritance
of height in humans.

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:37.320
I mean, at the same time,

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:39.441
developing the central limit theorem.

00:20:39.441 --> 00:20:40.403
And these were right,

00:20:40.403 --> 00:20:44.610
this is a figure one from a paper in PNAS.

00:20:44.610 --> 00:20:46.710
My understanding is that
historians of science

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:49.230
consider this the first path diagram.

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:53.550
He was trying to understand
what causes color code

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:56.130
in the Guinea pigs that he was studying.

00:20:56.130 --> 00:21:00.690
So late 19th century, early
20th century move forward

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:02.970
and you go to an extremely
influential paper

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:07.110
published by evolutionary
biologist, Ernst Mayr, at Harvard.

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:09.877
In 1961, he published a paper called,

00:21:09.877 --> 00:21:12.570
"Causes and Effects in Biology."

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:15.840
And what I wanna draw from
that paper to our two concepts

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:17.790
that I'm gonna come back as I discuss

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:20.160
how we try to do causal inference,

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:23.130
proximate causes and ultimate causes.

00:21:23.130 --> 00:21:25.410
Just think of those as
in proximate causes,

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:27.870
we're trying to understand
why things happens

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:29.910
in a mechanistic way, can be the cell,

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:31.800
can be the organ, can be the individual,

00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:34.710
you can think of physiology,
you can think of energetics,

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:37.230
you can think of endocrinology.

00:21:37.230 --> 00:21:40.950
Ultimate causes, Ernst Mayr
was developing this paper

00:21:40.950 --> 00:21:43.770
from what I read, in trying
to bring some balance

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:45.840
to what was a very strong tendency

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:48.660
to focus on genetics from
the twenties and thirties.

00:21:48.660 --> 00:21:52.020
Himself being an organismic
evolution biologist

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:54.900
thought that we also
needed to understand causes

00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:57.900
at an adaptive level, functional level.

00:21:57.900 --> 00:22:00.720
And that's what it's
been referred ever since

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:02.310
as ultimate causes.

00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:03.664
Why don't we come back,

00:22:03.664 --> 00:22:06.660
as I examine what progress have we made

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:09.540
in understanding proximate
and ultimate causes

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:12.690
for the phenomenon that
are of my interest.

00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:15.390
At that time, this is
the top panel of a figure

00:22:15.390 --> 00:22:17.280
of the paper that I show next.

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:18.990
You can see that the understanding there

00:22:18.990 --> 00:22:22.770
was that causality was
rather unidirectional,

00:22:22.770 --> 00:22:24.390
it was bottom up.

00:22:24.390 --> 00:22:27.450
You just go from the genome
in a very programmed way

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:30.510
to the cell, the organism,
and the environment.

00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:33.420
And whatever that happens
in that generation one,

00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:36.660
that's pretty much
inherited to generation two.

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:37.493
And that was set.

00:22:37.493 --> 00:22:41.006
And of course this has
changed over the years.

00:22:41.006 --> 00:22:43.560
And now, for many, many, for a long time,

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:46.770
we've developed
subdisciplines like evo-devo

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:49.530
where we understand that causality,

00:22:49.530 --> 00:22:51.780
we need to understand
causality at different levels.

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:56.340
Yes, there is an influence of
the genome in what comes next,

00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:58.680
but so there is influences of what happens

00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:01.050
at the level of the cell, of the organism,

00:23:01.050 --> 00:23:04.530
and the environment, and
it is not unidirectional,

00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:06.450
but it is reciprocal.

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:09.540
And some of these changes
that occurred during ontogeny,

00:23:09.540 --> 00:23:12.990
during your lifetime
may be also influencing

00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:14.670
the following generation.

00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:18.900
So we have moved away, most
of us, I would like to think,

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:20.730
from what all of you are familiar with,

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:23.490
which was so influential as well

00:23:23.490 --> 00:23:25.590
and with positive and
negative consequences

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:28.230
as portrayed in the
"The Selfish Gene" book

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:32.010
that Richard Dawkins wrote,
it's almost 50 years ago.

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:34.230
So that is what we call these days,

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:36.753
the extended evolutionary synthesis.

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:41.670
How do we do this within
biological anthropology?

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:43.770
In a nutshell, biological anthropology,

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:46.980
which we also call evolution anthropology,

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:48.570
physical anthropology,

00:23:48.570 --> 00:23:51.780
we are trying to understand
what it is, what it means

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:56.780
to be human if we take biology
and evolution into account.

00:23:57.000 --> 00:24:01.500
So when you study humans, of
course we have come to realize,

00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:04.290
embrace, include the component of culture.

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:07.410
You cannot understand
what it means to be humans

00:24:07.410 --> 00:24:09.270
if you to consider culture.

00:24:09.270 --> 00:24:12.210
But something that is, it's
a rather challenge these days

00:24:12.210 --> 00:24:16.050
to communicate in classroom is
that I am of the firm opinion

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:18.480
that everything begins with biology

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:21.540
because at the end of the
day, we are made of cells,

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:23.640
we are made of genes, et cetera.

00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:25.950
But you cannot ignore culture either.

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:27.750
So these are some questions that we ask

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:30.510
both on a very deep timescale

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:33.030
and also a very broad spatial scale.

00:24:33.030 --> 00:24:35.220
What makes us different
from other living organisms,

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:36.810
for example, chimps?

00:24:36.810 --> 00:24:39.120
When did we really start cooperating

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:41.820
for producing or obtaining the food

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:44.970
as the drawing on the top right shows you?

00:24:44.970 --> 00:24:48.780
And once we got that food,
when did we start making fire?

00:24:48.780 --> 00:24:51.210
Were we using the fire to warm ourselves?

00:24:51.210 --> 00:24:53.850
Were we using the fire to cook?

00:24:53.850 --> 00:24:54.870
And now that we cook,

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:59.870
suddenly our complete
nutritional functions change.

00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:02.370
When did we start walking on two legs?

00:25:02.370 --> 00:25:04.710
These are some typical classical questions

00:25:04.710 --> 00:25:09.030
that we ask in human biology,
in biological anthropology.

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:11.697
And then we go on to get data

00:25:11.697 --> 00:25:15.210
and we never get all the
data we would like to get.

00:25:15.210 --> 00:25:17.700
This is, by the way, I was thinking

00:25:17.700 --> 00:25:20.010
there's something I keep
telling students all the time

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:22.893
and I think that you'll
appreciate and agree with this.

00:25:23.910 --> 00:25:27.450
We forget that we are
never getting the data

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:28.950
we would like to get, right?

00:25:28.950 --> 00:25:31.527
We get what we can, not
what we would like to.

00:25:31.527 --> 00:25:33.660
And the students don't understand it.

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:35.730
And I sometimes I feel like we forget that

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:38.910
when we're reading papers
or reviewing grants.

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:41.460
The kind of evidence we can get, fossils.

00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:44.160
That is, I would say
that's rather unequivocal,

00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:46.350
we can hold a skull, right?

00:25:46.350 --> 00:25:47.880
We can hold a stone tool,

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:50.460
but we can quibble about who made it.

00:25:50.460 --> 00:25:53.520
Carrying and storing, we can
look at material culture.

00:25:53.520 --> 00:25:54.600
Genetics.

00:25:54.600 --> 00:25:56.190
There is information on genetics

00:25:56.190 --> 00:25:58.650
that can help us reconstruct the past.

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:00.840
Behavior, and that's my focus.

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:04.620
The catchphrase for behavior
is behavior doesn't fossilize.

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:07.410
And one of you showed
yesterday, I apologize,

00:26:07.410 --> 00:26:09.540
I think it was Thomas, the first slide

00:26:09.540 --> 00:26:12.120
was some footprints on a beach

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:14.910
and then we looked at a track of a fox.

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:16.680
Well, what do we make of that?

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:19.440
What do you make of that
if now you find a footprint

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:23.460
that you know has been dated
to 1.3 million years ago

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:25.050
in the savannas of Africa?

00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:26.643
You can make of that a lot.

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:31.110
How have we used these approaches

00:26:31.110 --> 00:26:33.210
and what are the kinds of
questions that are closer

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:35.010
to what I've been doing for this time?

00:26:35.010 --> 00:26:37.260
One of the things that
so many of us wonder

00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:41.640
is in what makes us humans,
what makes us love, right?

00:26:41.640 --> 00:26:44.490
Well, what are the proximate
and ultimate causes

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:47.880
that have developed a living organism

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:50.823
that can connect emotionally
with another living organism

00:26:50.823 --> 00:26:53.280
in a very peculiar way that we don't see

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:54.870
in many taxa, right?

00:26:54.870 --> 00:26:57.870
We refer to that
establishing some pair bonds.

00:26:57.870 --> 00:27:02.070
What is it that makes some
living primates develop

00:27:02.070 --> 00:27:04.931
these kind of emotional
bond in a very peculiar way

00:27:04.931 --> 00:27:08.280
with young, I'm not gonna
call them their offspring

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:10.377
for a while, we'll see,
we'll come back to that,

00:27:10.377 --> 00:27:12.720
and some of the living
organisms don't do it?

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:14.430
Paternal care.

00:27:14.430 --> 00:27:16.650
More specifically, we're narrowing down

00:27:16.650 --> 00:27:18.750
to the false side of my research.

00:27:18.750 --> 00:27:22.890
Why are some primates pair-living
and socially monogamous

00:27:22.890 --> 00:27:25.680
where some males such excellent fathers?

00:27:25.680 --> 00:27:28.620
Those are really the two central questions

00:27:28.620 --> 00:27:32.250
that I want to use to share some findings,

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:35.550
but probably even more than
that share some concerns,

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:38.730
some limitations, really embracing

00:27:38.730 --> 00:27:41.763
the uncertainty that there
is in all that I've done.

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:46.650
Much of the research that I'm synthesizing

00:27:46.650 --> 00:27:51.300
in my early 17 minutes
has done in collaboration

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:53.550
with hundreds of students, assistants,

00:27:53.550 --> 00:27:56.310
and some colleagues,
these, I wanted to mention.

00:27:56.310 --> 00:28:00.420
Anthony Di Fiore, we were
students together at UC Davis

00:28:00.420 --> 00:28:03.060
when we were grad students 10 years ago.

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:04.980
Marcelo Rotundo.

00:28:04.980 --> 00:28:07.500
You didn't pick up, you're
not paying attention.

00:28:07.500 --> 00:28:10.200
Marcelo Rotundo has been the
field manager in Argentina

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:11.580
working with me for 30 years.

00:28:11.580 --> 00:28:16.110
Maggie Corley and Alba
are two junior colleagues

00:28:16.110 --> 00:28:18.720
who have joined the
project now for some time.

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:22.140
And again, going back to when I said,

00:28:22.140 --> 00:28:23.370
listen, we need to do something.

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:28.150
So it was 18, 19, 2019 that we
started working on this paper

00:28:29.070 --> 00:28:31.470
that we published in the "Yearbook
of Physical Anthropology"

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:33.960
summarizing the research we've done

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:35.400
with some of these primates.

00:28:35.400 --> 00:28:38.310
In fact, it took us five years,

00:28:38.310 --> 00:28:40.560
no, it was published in 2020,

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:44.130
we started working on this on 2013.

00:28:44.130 --> 00:28:47.310
There was a science paper
published by one of my colleagues,

00:28:47.310 --> 00:28:50.850
one of the most famous
social evolution biologist,

00:28:50.850 --> 00:28:52.650
Tim Clutton-Brock at Cambridge,

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:55.593
making some claims about
the evolution of monogamy.

00:28:56.520 --> 00:29:00.930
And let's say we did not agree
with many of the conclusions

00:29:00.930 --> 00:29:03.510
and we just do not have
it in us to produce

00:29:03.510 --> 00:29:06.240
the quick turnaround to
refute some of the claims.

00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:08.100
It took us seven years.

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:11.340
It's the kind of work that
it's thorough, it's deep,

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:12.930
it doesn't make it into science,

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:15.090
you cannot summarize it in a tweet.

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:19.020
But we published a 60 page
paper that we're very proud of

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:23.400
because we really got into the
details of the issues we had

00:29:23.400 --> 00:29:25.140
with the inferences made

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:28.293
for the evolution of
social monogamy in mammals.

