for the evolution of a polygenous male?

00:41:43.170 --> 00:41:45.240
If the guy can cover 10 hectares,

00:41:45.240 --> 00:41:48.300
if he can monitor 10 hectares,
why don't just monitor

00:41:48.300 --> 00:41:51.153
two territories of five
instead of one of 10?

00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:54.420
The answer is we have no clue why not.

00:41:54.420 --> 00:41:56.970
So that's really after
28 years, there is no,

00:41:56.970 --> 00:42:00.090
I mean, it's very, very
difficult to resolve this.

00:42:00.090 --> 00:42:03.840
What we're trying to do now
is get into much more detail

00:42:03.840 --> 00:42:06.330
in measuring the
distribution of resources,

00:42:06.330 --> 00:42:08.013
which is extremely challenging.

00:42:09.390 --> 00:42:10.223
Why?

00:42:10.223 --> 00:42:11.490
I mean, some of these males,

00:42:11.490 --> 00:42:14.310
actually all of these males
are wonderful fathers.

00:42:14.310 --> 00:42:17.073
Why are some males excellent fathers?

00:42:17.073 --> 00:42:19.860
And that's a fascinating
question in evolutionary biology.

00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:20.913
Let me tell you why.

00:42:22.020 --> 00:42:23.220
You can connect with these guys

00:42:23.220 --> 00:42:25.050
being wonderful fathers, right?

00:42:25.050 --> 00:42:27.960
Male penguin keeps an eye on the chicks.

00:42:27.960 --> 00:42:30.300
Mom goes fishing and finding food.

00:42:30.300 --> 00:42:31.740
Seahorses, you probably know

00:42:31.740 --> 00:42:34.023
that the males hold the pregnancy.

00:42:36.300 --> 00:42:37.890
But mamas are very, very different.

00:42:37.890 --> 00:42:42.150
For one thing, fertilization
is inside the female tract

00:42:42.150 --> 00:42:44.610
and that generates father uncertainty.

00:42:44.610 --> 00:42:46.920
And second, mom, as opposed to the penguin

00:42:46.920 --> 00:42:50.250
and the seahorse, mom is
stuck with the investment,

00:42:50.250 --> 00:42:52.260
with the demand for energetics

00:42:52.260 --> 00:42:53.820
during pregnancy and lactation.

00:42:53.820 --> 00:42:57.690
So it is a puzzle in
mammalian reproductive biology

00:42:57.690 --> 00:43:00.750
why males would commit to a single female

00:43:00.750 --> 00:43:02.763
in a socially monogamous relationship.

00:43:05.370 --> 00:43:08.040
One possibility, we do know
that owl monkeys provide

00:43:08.040 --> 00:43:09.630
an awful lot of care.

00:43:09.630 --> 00:43:11.730
I mean, if you have children,
if you have grandchildren,

00:43:11.730 --> 00:43:13.380
encourage them to browse the web,

00:43:13.380 --> 00:43:14.910
to look at videos of owl monkeys

00:43:14.910 --> 00:43:17.370
taking care of the babies is fascinating.

00:43:17.370 --> 00:43:20.430
A couple days after the baby's born,

00:43:20.430 --> 00:43:22.620
the father is primarily involved

00:43:22.620 --> 00:43:24.840
in taking care of the
young, except for nursing,

00:43:24.840 --> 00:43:26.517
the male does everything.

00:43:26.517 --> 00:43:29.760
And we do feel based on
data from the lab as well,

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:32.850
that the infant has a stronger attachment

00:43:32.850 --> 00:43:34.290
to the father than the mother.

00:43:34.290 --> 00:43:36.813
That's remarkable for a mammal.

00:43:38.250 --> 00:43:39.480
They carry the infant,

00:43:39.480 --> 00:43:41.790
they share food with the
infant more than the mother.

00:43:41.790 --> 00:43:43.380
They groom, they touch, they play.

00:43:43.380 --> 00:43:46.620
They're the primary caregivers, of course,

00:43:46.620 --> 00:43:50.223
living outside nursing,
which is not trivial.

00:43:51.480 --> 00:43:53.430
We wanted to understand
what are some possible

00:43:53.430 --> 00:43:58.260
approximate causes of the
involvement of the father

00:43:58.260 --> 00:44:01.260
with the care of the young,
how that may be feeding

00:44:01.260 --> 00:44:04.470
and to explain pair bonding and monogamy.

00:44:04.470 --> 00:44:08.610
And again, on purpose, I
just pasted the whole text

00:44:08.610 --> 00:44:11.160
of this article because it
illustrates a little bit

00:44:11.160 --> 00:44:14.760
what I'm trying to get traction
on in my own discipline.

00:44:14.760 --> 00:44:16.650
Notice the paper is from 2021.

00:44:16.650 --> 00:44:21.150
And for three years, a
holiness of grant went on

00:44:21.150 --> 00:44:25.170
to collecting monkey shit
from the ground hoping that,

00:44:25.170 --> 00:44:27.120
but this is the path
we're trying to connect.

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:28.560
So what is the hypothesis?

00:44:28.560 --> 00:44:33.000
The hypothesis that for a
male to engage with a female,

00:44:33.000 --> 00:44:36.060
one possibility is that the
male is helping the female

00:44:36.060 --> 00:44:38.400
reduce the energetic burden.

00:44:38.400 --> 00:44:40.440
If I help you take care of a young,

00:44:40.440 --> 00:44:43.260
then you can rest, then you can sleep.

00:44:43.260 --> 00:44:45.780
So we wanna look at endocrinology,

00:44:45.780 --> 00:44:49.260
we wanna understand if
cortisol, which forget about,

00:44:49.260 --> 00:44:52.140
don't think about cortisol
anymore as a stress hormone,

00:44:52.140 --> 00:44:55.680
that's old, that's throw it
away, it's much more than that,

00:44:55.680 --> 00:44:58.320
and we're thinking of it
as a metabolic gatekeeper.

00:44:58.320 --> 00:45:00.900
Levels of cortisol are related

00:45:00.900 --> 00:45:03.780
to how we're metabolizing glucose, right?

00:45:03.780 --> 00:45:05.460
But that matters in the brain.

00:45:05.460 --> 00:45:08.610
I mean, we need to, if I
could get the data I wanted,

00:45:08.610 --> 00:45:11.520
I would like to know what's
happening with cortisol

00:45:11.520 --> 00:45:13.500
and cortisol receptors in the brain.

00:45:13.500 --> 00:45:14.333
I cannot do that.

00:45:14.333 --> 00:45:15.166
Well, can you do blood?

00:45:15.166 --> 00:45:16.110
No, I can't do that.

00:45:16.110 --> 00:45:16.943
Can you do urine?

00:45:16.943 --> 00:45:17.928
No, I can't do that.

00:45:17.928 --> 00:45:18.761
What can you do?

00:45:18.761 --> 00:45:20.610
Well, I can collect monkey
poop from the ground

00:45:20.610 --> 00:45:23.520
and hope that as a monkey excreted,

00:45:23.520 --> 00:45:24.480
whatever it excreted,

00:45:24.480 --> 00:45:27.600
something in the poop
gives me an adequate proxy

00:45:27.600 --> 00:45:29.280
of cortisol in the brain.

00:45:29.280 --> 00:45:30.113
We did that.

00:45:31.110 --> 00:45:32.970
This is an unusual note

00:45:32.970 --> 00:45:34.697
and section for our
journal and our discipline,

00:45:34.697 --> 00:45:36.210
but we just wanted to do it.

00:45:36.210 --> 00:45:40.140
And more and more, we're
doing multimodal selection,

00:45:40.140 --> 00:45:41.520
model averaging.

00:45:41.520 --> 00:45:44.280
But we can play with numbers all you want,

00:45:44.280 --> 00:45:46.560
what we cannot allow ourselves to forget

00:45:46.560 --> 00:45:49.020
is what is the quality,
the internal validity,

00:45:49.020 --> 00:45:52.470
the elasticity of the
variables that we have.

00:45:52.470 --> 00:45:55.020
And more and more, with my colleague

00:45:55.020 --> 00:45:57.570
and my wife, Claudia
Valeggia, we're feeling like,

00:45:57.570 --> 00:45:59.940
is it worth to put all this effort

00:45:59.940 --> 00:46:03.750
in trying to infer something
about the energetics

00:46:03.750 --> 00:46:07.353
of parental care from a measure
like the one we can get?

00:46:09.360 --> 00:46:12.060
Ultimate causation, what are
the benefits of paternal care?

00:46:12.060 --> 00:46:14.700
Huge survival of infants.

00:46:14.700 --> 00:46:16.590
We think that may be
part of the explanation

00:46:16.590 --> 00:46:18.060
of where we're seeing this.

00:46:18.060 --> 00:46:22.860
You do not get these rates of
survival in any other primate.

00:46:22.860 --> 00:46:26.400
For a wild animal, they
are doing wonderfully.

00:46:26.400 --> 00:46:28.290
And maybe it's associated with having

00:46:28.290 --> 00:46:30.393
both parents provide care.

00:46:34.110 --> 00:46:39.110
The other ultimate question
is, okay, if this is adaptive,

00:46:39.240 --> 00:46:41.370
if we can imagine this is
gonna evolve over time,

00:46:41.370 --> 00:46:43.380
the same way I was talking about

00:46:43.380 --> 00:46:47.730
why don't we see a polygenist
male strategy evolve?

00:46:47.730 --> 00:46:52.730
How come did we get to have a
male care strategy to evolve?

00:46:52.860 --> 00:46:54.780
For that, we need to understand

00:46:54.780 --> 00:46:56.730
if there's father uncertainty

00:46:56.730 --> 00:47:00.360
and if the males who are
keeping an eye on the females

00:47:00.360 --> 00:47:02.850
are really the ones who
are siring the young.

00:47:02.850 --> 00:47:06.540
It's crucial that we go from
describing their pattern

00:47:06.540 --> 00:47:09.540
as sexual monogamy, social monogamy,

00:47:09.540 --> 00:47:11.910
they're hanging together, sexual monogamy,

00:47:11.910 --> 00:47:15.060
they are copulating with
each other, genetic monogamy,

00:47:15.060 --> 00:47:20.010
they're really only producing
young with each other.

00:47:20.010 --> 00:47:24.720
And we know now that the system
we have, it's quite extreme

00:47:24.720 --> 00:47:27.180
because we have no other primate

00:47:27.180 --> 00:47:29.580
for which we have the kind of data we do.

00:47:29.580 --> 00:47:33.390
We have now tested 120 diets

00:47:33.390 --> 00:47:35.430
of putative fathers and offspring,

00:47:35.430 --> 00:47:39.600
and not a single time, we
have assigned the paternity

00:47:39.600 --> 00:47:42.720
to another male that it is
not the putative father.

00:47:42.720 --> 00:47:44.070
Most of you're familiar with the fact

00:47:44.070 --> 00:47:46.560
that when we assess paternity,

00:47:46.560 --> 00:47:49.590
we're not confirming the
identity of the father,

00:47:49.590 --> 00:47:51.330
we're simply just deciding

00:47:51.330 --> 00:47:54.480
which ones are not assigned paternity.

00:47:54.480 --> 00:47:56.280
So that may be another answer,

00:47:56.280 --> 00:47:58.620
partial answer to the evolution of this,

00:47:58.620 --> 00:48:02.220
that a male who is committing
to a monogamous relationship

00:48:02.220 --> 00:48:05.400
now succeeds in siring offspring

00:48:05.400 --> 00:48:08.970
that in turn probably have
that tendency to provide care

00:48:08.970 --> 00:48:13.140
as well and stay
pair-living and pair-bonded.

00:48:13.140 --> 00:48:17.400
So the idea is that maybe
you really have pair bonding

00:48:17.400 --> 00:48:21.150
as a necessary, but we don't
know if sufficient condition

00:48:21.150 --> 00:48:24.420
to then facilitate the
evolution of fatherhood

00:48:24.420 --> 00:48:25.713
and paternal care.

00:48:27.270 --> 00:48:29.070
You can read about all this.

00:48:29.070 --> 00:48:32.700
I added this because the
talk is gonna be public.

00:48:32.700 --> 00:48:35.637
So you can see the Yale Today
for the whole piece on this.

00:48:35.637 --> 00:48:38.970
And a book that we just
edited only this year,

00:48:38.970 --> 00:48:41.460
it's bringing together
30 years of research

00:48:41.460 --> 00:48:43.740
on owl monkeys all over Latin America.

00:48:43.740 --> 00:48:46.350
And you can really find
there are some good synthesis

00:48:46.350 --> 00:48:49.080
of the research that I have presented.

00:48:49.080 --> 00:48:51.420
This is the last slide with a list.

00:48:51.420 --> 00:48:53.550
I mean, really this is a
shopping list of the things

00:48:53.550 --> 00:48:57.390
that I would like our discipline
to consider some more.

00:48:57.390 --> 00:48:59.520
It is primarily observational.

00:48:59.520 --> 00:49:01.773
We normally have very few subjects.

00:49:02.790 --> 00:49:04.110
This is not for my discipline,

00:49:04.110 --> 00:49:06.720
but I noticed yesterday,
we didn't really talk,

00:49:06.720 --> 00:49:08.700
and I invite comments on this,

00:49:08.700 --> 00:49:10.710
we didn't really get in our conversations

00:49:10.710 --> 00:49:14.160
and presentations on what
differences are we gonna make

00:49:14.160 --> 00:49:17.010
when we're talking about
prediction for the individual

00:49:17.010 --> 00:49:19.154
and prediction for the population?

00:49:19.154 --> 00:49:21.150
I think we have a lot to discuss there

00:49:21.150 --> 00:49:24.930
because the public,
they're not getting it.

00:49:24.930 --> 00:49:27.870
They're confusing our
talking about we can model,

00:49:27.870 --> 00:49:30.060
we can predict, but we're not telling them

00:49:30.060 --> 00:49:31.923
this is not gonna help you.

00:49:33.270 --> 00:49:34.860
Sometimes, we have no clue about

00:49:34.860 --> 00:49:37.710
what are important effect
sizes that we wanna find

00:49:37.710 --> 00:49:39.420
or report or document.

00:49:39.420 --> 00:49:40.573
We literally have no clue.

00:49:40.573 --> 00:49:42.360
I don't know what is the
difference in cortisol

00:49:42.360 --> 00:49:43.920
as estimated from poop

00:49:43.920 --> 00:49:46.020
that really means something to the animal.

00:49:47.280 --> 00:49:49.050
There's no discussion in my discipline

00:49:49.050 --> 00:49:50.970
about the difference
between noise and bias.

00:49:50.970 --> 00:49:51.803
Actually, I think indeed,

00:49:51.803 --> 00:49:53.610
it's not happening in any field, right?

00:49:53.610 --> 00:49:54.690
Now, everything's a bias.

00:49:54.690 --> 00:49:56.280
Well, these are two different concepts.

00:49:56.280 --> 00:50:00.270
I recommend that book that
Daniel Cunningham just published.

00:50:00.270 --> 00:50:02.220
It's quite interesting to get the basics

00:50:02.220 --> 00:50:05.340
of the distinction between those two.

00:50:05.340 --> 00:50:06.330
Who was it yesterday?

00:50:06.330 --> 00:50:09.120
Samantha made me think, oh,
my goodness, missing data.

00:50:09.120 --> 00:50:10.710
Do we have work to do there?

00:50:10.710 --> 00:50:12.390
So there are things we cannot do,

00:50:12.390 --> 00:50:15.150
but all of these we can, let's do them.

00:50:15.150 --> 00:50:17.820
If you're missing data, think about them.

00:50:17.820 --> 00:50:20.160
Let me know why you didn't get those data.

00:50:20.160 --> 00:50:21.990
I mean, what are the
causes of missing data?

00:50:21.990 --> 00:50:23.010
That's huge.

00:50:23.010 --> 00:50:24.813
We're not doing it enough I think.

00:50:26.280 --> 00:50:28.650
For students, they confuse data

00:50:28.650 --> 00:50:30.723
and inference from data all the time.

00:50:31.770 --> 00:50:35.280
And then, all of this we
summarized in a commentary

00:50:35.280 --> 00:50:39.000
to a journal of our main society

00:50:39.000 --> 00:50:40.710
that we need to keep working.

00:50:40.710 --> 00:50:42.750
This is central to the
workshop on the difference

00:50:42.750 --> 00:50:46.143
between statistical inference
and causal inference.

00:50:47.160 --> 00:50:49.380
Thanks to all the agencies.

00:50:49.380 --> 00:50:53.597
And I thought that would be
a nice collage to finish.

00:50:53.597 --> 00:50:56.680
(attendees clapping)

00:51:00.576 --> 00:51:02.130
- [Priya] Thanks so much,
Eduardo, for opening eyes

00:51:02.130 --> 00:51:06.060
to very long causal chains that are needed

00:51:06.060 --> 00:51:08.280
in inference in your discipline.

00:51:08.280 --> 00:51:10.020
So we're open for questions.

00:51:10.020 --> 00:51:10.863
Yulia.

00:51:11.700 --> 00:51:13.260
Yako.

00:51:13.260 --> 00:51:14.670
- [Attendee 1] That was fascinating.

00:51:14.670 --> 00:51:17.430
So I learned that Barbary macaques

00:51:17.430 --> 00:51:19.590
are also excellent fathers,

00:51:19.590 --> 00:51:24.000
but the reason there is like
these groups like live together

00:51:24.000 --> 00:51:26.910
and because paternity's uncertain,

00:51:26.910 --> 00:51:28.950
the males look after all the children

00:51:28.950 --> 00:51:30.750
just in case they're theirs.

00:51:30.750 --> 00:51:34.680
And actually males form
bonds in Barbary macaques

00:51:34.680 --> 00:51:38.550
over like the looking after the young.

00:51:38.550 --> 00:51:41.310
So I mean, the result is the same,

00:51:41.310 --> 00:51:43.080
that they're very good fathers,

00:51:43.080 --> 00:51:47.280
but actually the relationship
they have with the females

00:51:47.280 --> 00:51:50.940
and the males have with each
other are very different.

00:51:50.940 --> 00:51:55.290
So I was wondering, you know,
like even in different types

00:51:55.290 --> 00:52:00.290
of creatures with maybe
similar type of habitat,

00:52:02.580 --> 00:52:07.530
similar type of resources
and so on, like, you know,

00:52:07.530 --> 00:52:09.700
like you get to the same outcome

00:52:12.369 --> 00:52:13.830
for very different reasons.

00:52:13.830 --> 00:52:16.740
And how can you untangle these things

00:52:16.740 --> 00:52:21.240
and how does more broadly,
is it clear what determines

00:52:21.240 --> 00:52:23.490
whether a group lives together

00:52:23.490 --> 00:52:26.700
and there's like, like a
bigger group lives together

00:52:26.700 --> 00:52:31.080
as a patriarchy or matriarchy or like,

00:52:31.080 --> 00:52:36.080
there's like, just like pairs
basically, like in this case?

00:52:36.090 --> 00:52:38.223
- So thanks.

00:52:39.240 --> 00:52:41.790
I'm reminding myself that
this is gonna be recorded

00:52:41.790 --> 00:52:44.250
and made public, but I'm
not gonna say anything

00:52:44.250 --> 00:52:46.560
that I haven't already
published in writing.

00:52:46.560 --> 00:52:48.900
So the Barbary macaques,
there's one single paper,

00:52:48.900 --> 00:52:51.004
empirical paper from the seventies,

00:52:51.004 --> 00:52:55.170
it was a relatively short
paper on Barbary macaques

00:52:55.170 --> 00:52:58.710
that again, and again gets
drawn into the same pool

00:52:58.710 --> 00:53:00.580
of all studies without distinguishing

00:53:01.770 --> 00:53:03.390
how robust the information is.

00:53:03.390 --> 00:53:04.980
My understanding is that Barbary macaques,

00:53:04.980 --> 00:53:08.310
some colleagues of mine have
studied to work on them again.

00:53:08.310 --> 00:53:11.160
But the outcomes are the same,

00:53:11.160 --> 00:53:13.770
but the extent to which the
questions have been examined,

00:53:13.770 --> 00:53:15.390
I don't think they are.

00:53:15.390 --> 00:53:17.970
So going to the Barbary, but
what matters what you said

00:53:17.970 --> 00:53:20.400
is I think it's
interesting that it is true

00:53:20.400 --> 00:53:22.890
that it's difficult to measure.

00:53:22.890 --> 00:53:25.140
We have tons of papers discussing

00:53:25.140 --> 00:53:28.410
how we're gonna define
paternal care, right?

00:53:28.410 --> 00:53:30.690
I mean, think about your own families.

00:53:30.690 --> 00:53:33.840
What does it mean that a pair of yours

00:53:33.840 --> 00:53:34.673
is taking care of you?

00:53:34.673 --> 00:53:38.640
More and more we are learning
with studies across societies

00:53:38.640 --> 00:53:41.070
or human societies that there could be

00:53:41.070 --> 00:53:42.870
a lot of observational learning

00:53:42.870 --> 00:53:45.120
that the young do from the father,

00:53:45.120 --> 00:53:48.360
but if we're looking at the
father, I mean if we didn't know

00:53:48.360 --> 00:53:51.840
that the son is there, we
would not be describing

00:53:51.840 --> 00:53:54.750
this adult male as teaching anything,

00:53:54.750 --> 00:53:57.570
but the child is learning from the father

00:53:57.570 --> 00:53:59.010
just through observation.

00:53:59.010 --> 00:54:00.660
So we make, in a very simplistic way,

00:54:00.660 --> 00:54:03.270
we talk about direct
care and indirect care.

00:54:03.270 --> 00:54:06.510
The Barbary macaque like every
single other primate I know,

00:54:06.510 --> 00:54:10.680
except for the titi monkeys do
not have in any way or manner

00:54:10.680 --> 00:54:14.310
the kind of direct
involvement with the young,

00:54:14.310 --> 00:54:15.690
transporting them, caring.

00:54:15.690 --> 00:54:20.550
I mean, the baby yells and
dad goes to its rescue.

00:54:20.550 --> 00:54:22.710
Now, it is possible, absolutely possible,

00:54:22.710 --> 00:54:26.520
but much harder to document
that the mere presence

00:54:26.520 --> 00:54:30.060
of adult males are providing
some extent of care

00:54:30.060 --> 00:54:34.020
because those males may be a
barrier to potential predators.

00:54:34.020 --> 00:54:37.230
Those males may be a
barrier to potential males

00:54:37.230 --> 00:54:39.000
who may kill those infants.

00:54:39.000 --> 00:54:40.530
So how we assess,

00:54:40.530 --> 00:54:43.500
then you brought up the word
bond, which I did as well.

00:54:43.500 --> 00:54:45.240
And that's a rabbit hole

00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:48.180
because again, how are
we going to define bond?

00:54:48.180 --> 00:54:49.920
We do not have.

00:54:49.920 --> 00:54:54.360
I use the word for our
research in the wild.

00:54:54.360 --> 00:54:57.780
But if you ask me, I mean,
I will say here on record

00:54:57.780 --> 00:55:00.060
that I don't know if
we have any solid data

00:55:00.060 --> 00:55:04.080
to speak about a bond for
the owl monkeys in Argentina.

00:55:04.080 --> 00:55:06.420
This is another way in which
in biological anthropology,

00:55:06.420 --> 00:55:08.640
we can make our inferences more robust.

00:55:08.640 --> 00:55:13.080
We can draw, we can use
the process of abduction.

00:55:13.080 --> 00:55:16.230
We need to do comparative biology

00:55:16.230 --> 00:55:18.780
because the way in which
I can do it is, okay,

00:55:18.780 --> 00:55:21.660
I don't know if a male
and female owl monkey,

00:55:21.660 --> 00:55:24.540
I don't know if the male
and the infant have a bond

00:55:24.540 --> 00:55:28.140
in the sense that if I
cut that relationship,

00:55:28.140 --> 00:55:29.640
there's gonna be distress.

00:55:29.640 --> 00:55:31.290
I haven't shown that.

00:55:31.290 --> 00:55:33.570
I haven't shown that
they will seek each other

00:55:33.570 --> 00:55:34.950
because they're always together.

00:55:34.950 --> 00:55:37.050
I cannot say parting them, right?

00:55:37.050 --> 00:55:38.700
But I'm started looking around

00:55:38.700 --> 00:55:40.770
and I see there are all kinds of studies

00:55:40.770 --> 00:55:44.130
spanning decades on all
kinds of other systems

00:55:44.130 --> 00:55:47.850
where when they see what I
see, they have also shown

00:55:47.850 --> 00:55:49.380
that there's a substrate,

00:55:49.380 --> 00:55:50.970
that there are proximate mechanisms,

00:55:50.970 --> 00:55:53.490
there's a physiology,
there's an endocrinology

00:55:53.490 --> 00:55:55.620
that speaks of a bond.

00:55:55.620 --> 00:55:58.080
We don't have that for Barbary macaques,

00:55:58.080 --> 00:56:00.300
technically speaking, we
don't have it for owl monkeys.

00:56:00.300 --> 00:56:03.807
The closest we get is to some
research on callitrichids

00:56:03.807 --> 00:56:05.520
and titi monkeys in captivity

00:56:05.520 --> 00:56:09.090
where there is some wonderful
work documenting the bonds.

00:56:09.090 --> 00:56:10.440
So how you get to that.

00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:12.390
Barbary macaques live in groups

00:56:12.390 --> 00:56:15.270
where you have several
males and several females,

00:56:15.270 --> 00:56:18.720
and again, we do not have
evidence that the relationship

00:56:18.720 --> 00:56:21.330
between males and females
in those groups is similar

00:56:21.330 --> 00:56:25.173
to the relationship you
have in pair-living taxa.

00:56:26.400 --> 00:56:29.370
But how we really make
progress in understanding

00:56:29.370 --> 00:56:32.700
how we got there, it's
profoundly difficult.

00:56:32.700 --> 00:56:36.030
I do have hopes in terms of the genetics,

00:56:36.030 --> 00:56:37.890
but the genetics then also,

00:56:37.890 --> 00:56:40.890
seeing how the genetics unfold, right?

00:56:40.890 --> 00:56:43.200
We need to make so much more progress

00:56:43.200 --> 00:56:46.380
in the black box between genes or genomes

00:56:46.380 --> 00:56:48.693
and these very, very complex phenotype.

00:56:50.190 --> 00:56:51.023
- [Priya] Yep.

00:56:51.960 --> 00:56:53.093
- [Attendee 2] Thank you.

00:56:54.330 --> 00:56:56.010
It's a wonderful topic.

00:56:56.010 --> 00:56:58.260
And I was wondering if you could
say a little bit more about

00:56:58.260 --> 00:57:01.290
the statistical norm that was
just mentioned in the paper

00:57:01.290 --> 00:57:04.500
that you wrote and whether
there have been discussions

00:57:04.500 --> 00:57:07.125
and people have been
questioning the statistic.

00:57:07.125 --> 00:57:08.250
- Statistical, I'm sorry,
- Norm.

00:57:08.250 --> 00:57:12.780
So in the paper, in the,
one more back is the, sorry,

00:57:12.780 --> 00:57:16.644
the one, there's a footnote
in your third to last slide.

00:57:16.644 --> 00:57:19.260
- P value.
- Yeah, the P value.

00:57:19.260 --> 00:57:20.220
- Oh.
- Yeah.

00:57:20.220 --> 00:57:21.570
- This one.
- Yeah.

00:57:21.570 --> 00:57:22.628
- Yes.

00:57:22.628 --> 00:57:23.461
- Can you say a little
bit more about that?

00:57:23.461 --> 00:57:25.500
- Yes, absolutely, don't get me started.

00:57:25.500 --> 00:57:27.750
- Oh, well maybe you
shouldn't say something.

00:57:28.650 --> 00:57:29.983
- I'd love to, I have to.

00:57:29.983 --> 00:57:32.160
- Briefly comment.
- I have two minutes.

00:57:32.160 --> 00:57:34.950
Already in the nineties, I
was trained by psychologists,

00:57:34.950 --> 00:57:36.330
they knew about meta-analysis,

00:57:36.330 --> 00:57:38.130
there wasn't anything new about it, right?

00:57:38.130 --> 00:57:39.600
They knew about meta-analysis.

00:57:39.600 --> 00:57:42.480
But already, back when I was
a grad student, I realized

00:57:42.480 --> 00:57:44.850
that now hypothesis significance testing

00:57:44.850 --> 00:57:48.390
was profoundly inadequate
for everything I was doing.

00:57:48.390 --> 00:57:51.468
And still, I still have
tables full of stars.

00:57:51.468 --> 00:57:54.630
It looks like a cosmology experiment.

00:57:54.630 --> 00:57:58.770
So now, in 2021, again,
through this period, we decided

00:57:58.770 --> 00:58:01.230
that we wanted to share
with our colleagues,

00:58:01.230 --> 00:58:03.990
but this was because I was
following with my colleagues

00:58:03.990 --> 00:58:06.000
at the American Statistical Association

00:58:06.000 --> 00:58:08.220
and the statement that the
American Statistical Association

00:58:08.220 --> 00:58:13.220
published in 2016 saying
misuse and use of p-values,

00:58:13.290 --> 00:58:15.690
they needed to include
the use in the title.

00:58:15.690 --> 00:58:19.500
But the bottom line is 99% of
people are misusing p-values

00:58:19.500 --> 00:58:22.020
and statistical significance
followed by the nature paper

00:58:22.020 --> 00:58:25.650
with a hundred signatories
saying, let's stop doing this.

00:58:25.650 --> 00:58:29.580
And I'm begging, you stop doing it

00:58:29.580 --> 00:58:31.440
because I think it's made so much damage

00:58:31.440 --> 00:58:34.350
for most research situations.

00:58:34.350 --> 00:58:36.180
Definitely I'm speaking for ours.

00:58:36.180 --> 00:58:38.130
In biological anthropology,

00:58:38.130 --> 00:58:39.780
there are seldom any opportunities

00:58:39.780 --> 00:58:41.850
where you're gonna make progress,

00:58:41.850 --> 00:58:43.710
definitely not in causal inference

00:58:43.710 --> 00:58:47.850
by deciding whether this
is worth reporting or not,

00:58:47.850 --> 00:58:50.970
based on an arbitrate dichotomous cutoff,

00:58:50.970 --> 00:58:53.850
that it's the same no
matter what you're studying.

00:58:53.850 --> 00:58:56.340
So again, I'll go on record.

00:58:56.340 --> 00:58:57.660
What was the reaction?

00:58:57.660 --> 00:59:00.030
That it was the first time in my life

00:59:00.030 --> 00:59:03.270
that the two reviewers said
this has to be published

00:59:03.270 --> 00:59:06.054
and the editor decided
to send it to a third

00:59:06.054 --> 00:59:08.130
and the third said, this
has to be published.

00:59:08.130 --> 00:59:11.073
And the editor eventually,
I guess she published it.

00:59:12.720 --> 00:59:15.390
But why don't we see traction?

00:59:15.390 --> 00:59:18.780
I think it's because we need
to train our younger colleagues

00:59:18.780 --> 00:59:20.400
to take responsibility.

00:59:20.400 --> 00:59:21.690
You are the expert.

00:59:21.690 --> 00:59:23.910
We need to go back to domain knowledge.

00:59:23.910 --> 00:59:27.780
I think it was Samantha
again, the easiest is,

00:59:27.780 --> 00:59:28.920
no, I don't know what this means,

00:59:28.920 --> 00:59:31.800
but the piece 0.04, it
ought to be important.

00:59:31.800 --> 00:59:34.500
That's what we're getting
from students a lot.

00:59:34.500 --> 00:59:35.700
And they're not thinking.

00:59:35.700 --> 00:59:38.730
I go back to my second slide.

00:59:38.730 --> 00:59:41.130
You have to know your discipline.

00:59:41.130 --> 00:59:44.280
You have to think hard,
you have to think deep,

00:59:44.280 --> 00:59:46.200
you have to be open and modest

00:59:46.200 --> 00:59:50.490
with what you hope you can
accomplish and all that,

00:59:50.490 --> 00:59:54.810
it's absolutely counter to
structuring your findings

00:59:54.810 --> 00:59:57.690
and publications around an arbitrary.

00:59:57.690 --> 00:59:59.820
For me, it's mind blowing
that we're still using 0.05

01:00:01.227 --> 01:00:02.730
and we haven't.

01:00:02.730 --> 01:00:04.920
So there was a lot.

01:00:04.920 --> 01:00:08.133
But I'm optimistic, I'm
optimistic we're making progress.

01:00:10.230 --> 01:00:11.730
- Two quick questions.

01:00:11.730 --> 01:00:13.733
- [Attendee 3] Oh, one
quick question, yeah.

01:00:14.820 --> 01:00:16.476
Okay, sorry.

01:00:16.476 --> 01:00:18.120
So I just, I have one.

01:00:18.120 --> 01:00:21.840
So one, the question is,
what inferences do you have

01:00:21.840 --> 01:00:25.560
about how your monkeys choose their mates?

01:00:25.560 --> 01:00:28.233
Or to put it differently,
what's the nature of love?

01:00:30.540 --> 01:00:32.640
- The nature of love, the nature of love-

01:00:32.640 --> 01:00:33.540
- [Priya] Very quick answer.

01:00:33.540 --> 01:00:35.190
- Right, the nature of love, I suggest,

01:00:35.190 --> 01:00:37.380
let's talk about it
later, but the inference,

01:00:37.380 --> 01:00:40.590
how they choose, that's
where you need a lab.

01:00:40.590 --> 01:00:42.060
That's where you can really have,

01:00:42.060 --> 01:00:45.900
we have a lot of expertise in
how we can assess preference,

01:00:45.900 --> 01:00:48.810
be of mates, be of food,
anything you choose.

01:00:48.810 --> 01:00:50.820
So we can do that in the lab.

01:00:50.820 --> 01:00:54.750
We haven't yet, because the
challenge is, even if we do it

01:00:54.750 --> 01:00:57.450
with a very carefully
crafted study design,

01:00:57.450 --> 01:01:00.120
we still have relatively
low number of subjects.

01:01:00.120 --> 01:01:02.370
And so, and I suspect that the answers

01:01:02.370 --> 01:01:03.720
are gonna be all over the place.

01:01:03.720 --> 01:01:05.220
It'll have to be done in the lab

01:01:05.220 --> 01:01:09.510
and that's as much as I can
say with the time we have.

01:01:09.510 --> 01:01:11.790
I'd love to, let's sit down,

01:01:11.790 --> 01:01:13.950
we can all contribute the
same about the nature of love.

01:01:13.950 --> 01:01:15.570
I mean, I'm no expert there.

01:01:15.570 --> 01:01:16.403
Thank you.

